A **jump discontinuity** happens when the two branches reach different values at $x = a$, so the graph steps.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <-] (-0.6,0.85) -- (2.4,3.2);
\draw[thick, ->] (2.4,1.3) -- (5.2,2.3);
\fill (2.4,3.2) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[fill=white] (2.4,1.3) circle (2.5pt);
\draw (2.4,0.12) -- (2.4,-0.12);
\node[below] at (2.4,-0.12) {$a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A **removable discontinuity** is a hole, where the curve reaches one value from both sides but the point itself is missing.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (5.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-0.6) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick, <->] (-0.5,0.7) -- (5.2,4.12);
\draw[fill=white] (2.4,2.44) circle (2.5pt);
\draw (2.4,0.12) -- (2.4,-0.12);
\node[below] at (2.4,-0.12) {$a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An **infinite discontinuity** is a vertical asymptote, where the function values grow without bound near $x = a$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (5.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.2) -- (0,3.4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[dashed] (2.4,-3.1) -- (2.4,3.3);
\draw[thick, <->, domain=-0.6:2.05, smooth, samples=60, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {1/(\x-2.4)});
\draw[thick, <->, domain=2.75:5, smooth, samples=60, variable=\x] plot ({\x}, {1/(\x-2.4)});
\node[below right] at (2.4,0) {$a$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

